&ldquo;You will find them where those came from,&rdquo; he said, &ldquo;and everything else
      your appetites call for; and you need not name the thing you wish; as long
      as I am with you, you have only to wish and find.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, all these matters are a secret among us four. I do not mind your
      trying to tell them, if you like, but I will protect your tongues, and
      nothing of the secret will escape from them.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, one perceives it now,&rdquo; said Nikolaus, &ldquo;but we are not spirits. It is
      plain he did not see you, but were we invisible, too? He looked at us, but
      he didn't seem to see us.&rdquo;

&ldquo;And I you. And as a proof of it I will show you something fine to see.
      Usually when I go I merely vanish; but now I will dissolve myself and let
      you see me do it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;That is good. I came the same way. I have lost my wallet. There wasn't
      much in it, but a very little is much to me, for it was all I had. I
      suppose you haven't seen anything of it?&rdquo;

It threw some light, but not a glare, and I was a little disappointed,
      also to some degree embarrassed. He was waiting for me to go on, so, in
      default of anything else to say, I asked, &ldquo;Is it valuable?&rdquo;

It would not have been safe for us to say he was mistaken, but we knew he
      was. Nikolaus said, &ldquo;We ask pardon for the mistake, but we meant to say
      seven.&rdquo;

Old Ursula's jaws worked, but she could not get any word out for the
      moment, she was so horrified. When she got her tongue, she stormed out,
      &ldquo;Go about your business, you puppy, or I will take a stick to you!&rdquo;

The creature answered to the name and came. Ursula examined its tongue.
      &ldquo;Upon my word, it's true!&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;I have not seen this kind of a cat
      before. Is it yours?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don't mean sell it. I mean have an income from it. This kind is called
      the Lucky Cat. Its owner finds four silver groschen in his pocket every
      morning.&rdquo;

Before Marget could speak, Satan had the word, and was talking back to
      Ursula in her own language&mdash;which was a surprise to her, and for her
      mistress, too. He said, &ldquo;Didn't I see you down the road awhile ago?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Ah, that pleases me; I see you remember me.&rdquo; He stepped to her and
      whispered: &ldquo;I told you it is a Lucky Cat. Don't be troubled; it will
      provide.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; said Satan indifferently; &ldquo;some even call him a Prince, out of
      compliment, but he is not bigoted; to him personal merit is everything,
      rank nothing.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Money!&rdquo; he said; &ldquo;they've got plenty of it. They pay me two groschen a
      week, besides my keep. And they live on the fat of the land, I can tell
      you; the prince himself can't beat their table.&rdquo;

&ldquo;They have not ventured to lay their hands upon an anointed servant of God
      before,&rdquo; he said; &ldquo;and how they could have dared it this time I cannot
      make out, for he wore his crucifix. Isn't it so?&rdquo;

&ldquo;The people will realize this,&rdquo; said Nikolaus's father, the judge, &ldquo;and
      despair will take away their courage and their energies. We have indeed
      fallen upon evil times.&rdquo;

He sighed, and Wohlmeyer said, in a troubled voice: &ldquo;The report of it all
      will go about the country, and our village will be shunned as being under
      the displeasure of God. The Golden Stag will know hard times.&rdquo;

I fell asleep to pleasant music that night&mdash;the patter of rain upon
      the panes and the dull growling of distant thunder. Away in the night
      Satan came and roused me and said: &ldquo;Come with me. Where shall we go?&rdquo;

&ldquo;What? I do random things? Indeed, I never do. I stop and consider
      possible consequences? Where is the need? I know what the consequences are
      going to be&mdash;always.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, it is true that they are nothing to me. It is not possible that
      they should be. The difference between them and me is abysmal,
      immeasurable. They have no intellect.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh yes, everybody does. My mother says she is so sweet and so lovely that
      she is not like any other child. She says she will be the pride of the
      village when she grows up; and its idol, too, just as she is now.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, Satan! Oh, dear Satan!&rdquo; I cried, with the tears rising in my eyes,
      &ldquo;save them! Don't let it happen. I can't bear to lose Nikolaus, he is my
      loving playmate and friend; and think of Lisa's poor mother!&rdquo;

&ldquo;His happiness will not proceed from that cause. I shall change his life
      that day, for his good. He will never know his good name has been
      restored.&rdquo;

&ldquo;His father is out of all patience with these goings-on, and will not have
      any more of it. Half the time when Nick is needed he is not to be found;
      then it turns out that he has been gadding around with you two. His father
      gave him a flogging last night. It always grieved me before, and many's
      the time I have begged him off and saved him, but this time he appealed to
      me in vain, for I was out of patience myself.&rdquo;

She was ironing at the time, and her back was partly toward me. She turned
      about with a startled or wondering look in her face and said, &ldquo;What do you
      mean by that?&rdquo;

&ldquo;All day. It's such a pity, too; it's a beautiful day, and he is so unused
      to being shut up. But he is busy planning his party, and maybe that is
      company for him. I do hope he isn't too lonesome.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Sit down,&rdquo; he said, &ldquo;and look at what I've been doing. And I've finished
      a kite that you will say is a beauty. It's drying, in the kitchen; I'll
      fetch it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No. If I had that power would any of you holy hypocrites be alive five
      minutes? No; I would strike you all dead. Pronounce your sentence and let
      me go; I am tired of your society.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Very well,&rdquo; he said; &ldquo;would you like to see a history of the progress of
      the human race?&mdash;its development of that product which it calls
      civilization?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now,&rdquo; said Satan, &ldquo;you have seen your progress down to the present, and
      you must confess that it is wonderful&mdash;in its way. We must now
      exhibit the future.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Three witnesses,&rdquo; said Satan: &ldquo;Mueller, the blacksmith; Klein, the
      butcher's man; Pfeiffer, the weaver's journeyman. Three very ordinary
      liars. Are there any more?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Never mind whether there are others or not, and never mind about what you
      consider us&mdash;three's enough to settle your matter for you. You'll
      prove that you threw a stone, or it shall go hard with you.&rdquo;

&ldquo;And first you will answer that other question,&rdquo; cried the blacksmith,
      pleased with himself for being mouthpiece to the public and hero of the
      occasion. &ldquo;What are you laughing at?&rdquo;

So we walked away, and I was not at ease, but was saying to myself, &ldquo;He
      told them he was laughing at them, but it was a lie&mdash;he was laughing
      at me.&rdquo;

&ldquo;And the money so found was never out of his hands thenceforth up to a
      certain definite date&mdash;the last day of last year. Correct me, sir, if
      I am wrong.&rdquo;

Well, what Satan had done was this: he had appeared before that poor
      prisoner, exclaiming, &ldquo;The trial is over, and you stand forever disgraced
      as a thief&mdash;by verdict of the court!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Don't do it, dear; remember, there are witnesses, and it is not becoming
      in the Crown Princess. Tell me your trouble&mdash;it shall be mended;
      there is nothing the Emperor cannot do.&rdquo; Then he looked around and saw old
      Ursula with her apron to her eyes. He was puzzled at that, and said, &ldquo;And
      what is the matter with you?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Please let them have their pleasure for an hour, sir&mdash;only that, and
      no longer. Afterward you may forbid them; and you will still have more
      fruit than you and the state together can consume in a year.&rdquo;

&ldquo;He? He will not trust that secret with any one; he will reflect that it
      could be revealed in sleep, in the hearing of some Portuguese guest's
      servant some time or other.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, and anywhere in Europe he would burn the man, too. But in India the
      people are civilized, and these things will not happen. The man will drive
      the priest away and take care of the tree.&rdquo;

A gust of thankfulness rose in my breast, but a doubt checked it before it
      could issue in words, and I said, &ldquo;But&mdash;but&mdash;we have seen that
      future life&mdash;seen it in its actuality, and so&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Nothing exists; all is a dream. God&mdash;man&mdash;the world&mdash;the
      sun, the moon, the wilderness of stars&mdash;a dream, all a dream; they
      have no existence. Nothing exists save empty space&mdash;and you!&rdquo;

&ldquo;The cat lied. There was nothing in that hole but an ass. There wasn't a
      sign of a flat thing visible. It was a handsome ass, and friendly, but
      just an ass, and nothing more.&rdquo;

&ldquo;This is very strange,&rdquo; said the elephant; &ldquo;the cat was always truthful
      before&mdash;as far as we could make out. Let another witness try. Go,
      Baloo, look in the hole, and come and report.&rdquo;
